realizes → Design Scenario (§2.1, pending Step 9); serves → OBJ-01…OBJ-05; uses → TDP, IDP (pending Steps 12-13); asserts → UC01-F1-PR1, UC01-F2-PR2, UC01-F3-PR3, UC01-F4-PR4, UC01-F2-CL1…UC01-F4-CL8.

= Use Case  [UC02] =

//Declared and deliberately deferred, so the scope decision is explicit rather than implicit in what happened to get written first.//

**Report Format:** §2.3 Objectives, Use Cases, Functions, Premises and Claims

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|UC02
|Name|Support the resident through the medication routine
|Status|Not Started
|Evidence basis|Would formalize SA-02 and PS-02. No primary data collected by this project.
|Scope decision|(((
SA-02 and SA-01 have different triggers and different Goals, so this is a separate Use Case rather than an alternate flow of UC01 - it is not a candidate for merging.

It is deferred for this cycle rather than dropped. Reasons:

- PS-02's out-of-scope field already narrows it heavily, including a care-home blacklist for residents on critical medication, which needs settling before Functions can be written.

- PS-02 adds a fourth sub-problem, confirmation, that has no counterpart in PS-01 - the resident failing to confirm they have taken the medication, risking a double dose. That needs its own Function and its own Claim, and possibly a safety Premise, rather than reusing F1-F4.

- Specifying it properly would roughly double the Premise and Claim count, and §1.2 Measures are not yet written for the first set.

F1-F4 are expected to transfer, since both activities are stepwise sequences with initiation, sequencing and completion breakdowns. Confirming that recurrence across two Use Cases is what would justify abstracting the TDP and IDP in Steps 12-13 - so UC02 should be written before those are finalized, not after.
)))

= Open items =

These are gaps recorded as explicit decisions rather than left silent, per Step 14.

|=ID|=Item|=Blocks|=Owner
|O-1|§1.2 Measures and Evaluation Methods are still empty templates. M-01…M-07 and EM-01…EM-03 are drafted below and cited throughout the tables above, but they are not yet Foundation entities. Anyone else writing Measures should start at M-08.|Every Verification and Validation Criteria cell above|§1.2 owner
|O-2|VT-01's non-negotiable floor requires the System to notify caregivers on immediate risk of serious harm. PS-02's out-of-scope field calls relaying an emergency "likely out of scope". Both cannot stand.|Action Sequence step 10; F3's scope; UC01-F3-PR3|Group decision
|O-3|F4 has no Objective it can be traced to. Either SA-01 needs a Goal covering care continuity and handover, or F4 is out of scope this cycle.|F4|Group decision
|O-4|Resolved. UC01-F2-CL5 previously rested on a Human Value with no supporting concept. Kitwood (1997) supplies it - infantilisation and outpacing are named detractors of personhood in dementia care. Remaining action: §1.2 should add this as an HFC so the Claim grounds on a concept rather than on a Claim-level citation.|Nothing|§1.2 owner
|O-5|§1.3 Technology Options is an empty template. The robot decision is recorded only incidentally, in PS-02's out-of-scope field.|All [pending §1.3] thresholds; the "Enabled by" clause on F1-F4|§1.3 owner
|O-6|The HV IDs on the §1.1 Stakeholders page do not match the §1.2 Human Values page. Every Grounding cell above uses the §1.2 numbering.|Traceability of every Grounding cell|§1.1 owner
|O-7|(((
F2's direction is contested by the literature. F2 is least-to-most: allow the stall, prompt minimally, then escalate. The dementia ADL literature largely favours the opposite - errorless learning with vanishing cues, prompting early to prevent the error and fading support across sessions (de Werd et al., 2013). The largest trial of that approach, REDALI-DEM (Voigt-Radloff et al., 2017; n = 161, nine 1-hour sessions over eight weeks), found that structured relearning improved ADL performance and that improvements held for six months - but that errorless learning gave no additional effect over trial-and-error learning. Note that de Werd is an author on both the review and the trial that failed to confirm it.

Two things follow. First, the evidence neither endorses F2's direction nor rules it out. Second, REDALI-DEM's participants had Alzheimer's or mixed-type dementia and were living at home, so even that null result transfers to care-home residents only cautiously.

Either F2 is re-specified as most-to-least with fading, or it stays as a deliberate bet that UC01-F2-CL4 is designed to settle - which has to be stated as a bet rather than presented as established.
)))|F2's specification; CL1, CL4|Group decision
|O-8|(((
Self-report of autonomy in this population is unreliable, and this is measured, not just suspected. Custers et al. (2013) compared nursing-home residents' self-reported fulfilment of the needs for relatedness, autonomy and competence against trained neutral observers rating videotaped caregiving episodes. Residents rated their own need fulfilment higher than observers did, with only weak-to-moderate agreement between the two, and only the //in general// self-report related to self-reported well-being.

So UC01-F2-CL3 cannot rest on M-05 alone: a favourable M-05 result may just be the ceiling. Custers et al. also supplies the fix, since it measures need fulfilment three ways - in general, in the caring relationship, and during a specific videotaped caregiving episode. The third is the right shape for M-05, because it is episode-specific rather than global, and it comes with an observer counterpart.

Remaining caveat: that sample was physically frail residents (n = 36, mean age 80), not specifically residents with dementia.
)))|M-05; UC01-F2-CL3|§1.2 owner
|O-9|UC01-F3-CL6 asserts no increase in care-worker time, but Rogers et al. (1999) found graduated prompting took considerably more caregiver time than usual care. The System is meant to absorb that, which is the point - but the Claim should probably be split into completion effect and time effect rather than asserting both at once.|UC01-F3-CL6|Mine, pending group view
|O-10|(((
Resolved. The earlier TODO asked whether being prompted by a //robot// specifically, rather than a screen or a voice, changes the infantilisation effect in CL5. It does.

Furlanis et al. (2026) argue that social robots are "socially legible bodies, interpreted in context through posture, timing, tone, and spatial behavior", and give three dimensions along which bias is enacted: embodied stereotypes (appearance), embodied constraints (affordances), and embodied treatment - bias arising in real-time interaction through gaze, timing and tone. F2's prompt pacing falls in the third. Their illustrative case includes a robot that exaggerated slowness and gentleness toward older adults, which was read as condescending rather than accommodating, which is CL5's mechanism. Note this paper is explicitly conceptual, so it frames the risk rather than measuring it.

The empirical source they cite for this is Lee et al. (2016), "That robot is not for me: Addressing stereotypes of aging in assistive robot design."

Form also changes the role the robot gets assigned. Pemberton et al. (2026) interviewed 13 older adults and found they sort socially assistive robots into four roles by autonomy and relationship tolerance - Carer, Pet, Warden, Tool. Humanoid robots were assigned the "Warden" role: oversight without attachment. Older adults preferred "tool" or "pet" framings over "friend" or "assistant" to protect dignity and autonomy, and (reported there from Prakash & Rogers, 2015) preferred more mechanical-looking robots that preserve emotional distance in private contexts such as bathing. Tooth brushing is such a context.

So CL5 and CL7 are both sensitive to the §1.3 choice, and in the same direction: a humanoid, expressive form raises the infantilisation risk in CL5 //and// the being-watched risk in CL7. See Open item O-11.

Transfer limits: Pemberton et al. studied cognitively intact older adults, in a VR home setup, ageing in place - not care-home residents with dementia.
)))|CL5, CL7; feeds §1.3|Closed

|O-11|(((
Arising from O-10. The evidence points somewhere specific on the open embodiment question: for a personal-care activity, a less humanlike, appliance-like form that preserves emotional distance is better supported than an expressive humanoid one. Zsiga et al. (2013, reported in Pemberton et al., 2026) also found older adults name overreach in control and cognitive support as key barriers to acceptance - which is this System's exact category.

Two actions: (a) §1.3 should record this as the rationale for whichever Technology Option is selected, and as the reason for rejecting a humanoid one; (b) §1.2 should add an HFC on embodiment and perceived role, so CL5 grounds on a concept rather than on Claim-level citations. That would also close the remaining half of O-4.
)))|§1.3's selection rationale; CL5's grounding|§1.3 and §1.2 owners

== Measures and Evaluation Methods these tables require ==

//Drafted here so the tables above are not citing nothing. These belong on the §1.2 pages, not on this one - move them there and delete this section. Where a published instrument exists it is named, so these are selections rather than inventions.//

|=ID|=Construct|=Instrument|=Type|=Serves|=Human subjects
|M-01|Step-tracking and stall-detection performance - correct step identification, stall-detection latency, spurious-prompt rate|Staged-event protocol against a ground-truth log. Bespoke, but the protocol follows COACH's system-performance reporting (Mihailidis et al., 2008).|Quantitative|Verification|No
|M-02|Independent step completion - SA-01 steps completed with no prompt, per session|Step-level scoring as used in COACH (Mihailidis et al., 2008): one point per step completed without human assistance. Directly reusable, not adapted.|Quantitative|Validation|Yes
|M-03|Prompt level required - highest level reached per step: none, orienting, naming, demonstration, care-worker takeover|Bespoke ordinal coding. Levels correspond to SA-01's own recorded workarounds and to the assistance levels used in Rogers et al. (1999).|Quantitative, ordinal|Validation|Yes
|M-04|Observed distress and resistance during the activity|(((
Resistiveness to Care Scale (Mahoney et al., 1999) - 13 observed behaviours, α .82-.87, developed specifically for ADL care episodes in advanced dementia, with later reliability and validity support (Galik et al., 2017). Validated, not invented.

Optionally paired with the Observed Emotion Rating Scale for affect - psychometrics in Lawton et al. (1996). Burden note: Lawton et al. (1999) found that reliability was "less than desirable" when the scale was used by certified nursing assistants rather than trained research staff, so if care staff rather than we do the rating, training time has to be budgeted.
)))|Observational, coded|Validation|Yes
|M-05|Perceived autonomy and felt control - the M-xx placeholder HFC-01 already points at|(((
Use the episode-specific items from Custers et al. (2013), which measured nursing-home residents' fulfilment of the autonomy, competence and relatedness needs during a specific videotaped caregiving episode - the right granularity for a single tooth-brushing session.

Must be paired with the observer rating, not used alone: the same study found residents rate their need fulfilment higher than observers do, with only weak-to-moderate agreement. See Open item O-8.
)))|Quantitative, plus observer counterpart|Validation|Yes
|M-06|Perceived privacy and monitoring acceptability - the M-xx placeholder HFC-02 already points at|Interview coding for expressions of being watched, plus care-staff judgement of what the record exposes. Bespoke, but the concern set is drawn from Niemeijer et al. (2010, 2015).|Mixed|Validation|Yes
|M-07|Care-worker prompting load - count and duration of prompting episodes per session|Time-sampled observation, as in Rogers et al. (1999), which reported proportion of a morning care session spent on physical assists.|Quantitative|Validation|Yes

|=ID|=Evaluation Method|=Purpose|=Measures|=Known limitation
|EM-01|Staged-session verification against a ground-truth log. Scripted stalls, resumptions, and confounders at a mock sink.|Verification|M-01, M-06 (record content)|Cannot establish real-world accuracy with an actual resident in an actual bathroom.
|EM-02|Observed within-resident comparison during the real morning routine, against each resident's own pre-System baseline. Design follows the single-subject alternating baseline-intervention structure used in COACH (Mihailidis et al., 2008).|Validation|M-02, M-03, M-04, M-05, M-07|Small n, no control group, cannot separate novelty effects from the design. High ethics load - dementia participants, supported consent.
|EM-03|Care-staff interview on the handover record and on perceived intrusion.|Validation|M-06, M-07|Cannot establish the resident's own privacy experience - only how staff read it.

